                        "type": "0",
                        "version": 0,
                        "content": "The nurse is analyzing the posthemodialysis laboratory test results for a client with chronic kidney disease. The nurse interprets that the dialysis is having an expected but nontherapeutic effect if which value is decreased?",
                        "subjectId": "ad5ab04c79144c0ea9b83385308c3b1d",
                        "difficultyIds": "a0f7d178f7b728b8bba7ef20ddeca62d",
                        "difficulty": "考试范围-Evaluating 评价",
                        "textAnalysis": "Rationale:\nHemodialysis typically lowers the amounts of fluid, sodium, potassium, urea nitrogen, creatinine, uric acid, magnesium, and phosphate levels in the blood. Hemodialysis also worsens anemia because RBCs are lost during dialysis from blood sampling and anticoagulation and from residual blood left in the dialyzer. Although all of these results are expected, only the lowered RBC count is nontherapeutic and worsens the anemia already caused by the disease process.",
                        "id": "1926ed845cac4d73ac22645973d94b97",
                        "mark": "0",
                        "collectionState": "0"
                    },
                    {
                        "optionList": [
                            {
                                "isRight": "0",
                                "userAnswer": "",
                                "isSelected": "0",
                                "id": "b1de32e20e124e82b0bc478e8e10e64b",
                                "content": "The increase in urine output indicates the return of some renal function."
                            },
                            {
                                "isRight": "0",
                                "userAnswer": "",
                                "isSelected": "0",
                                "id": "a14168785aa748659a4f438d70fc0f26",
                                "content": "The diuretic phase develops about 14 days after the initial insult and lasts about 10 days."
                            },
                            {
                                "isRight": "0",
                                "userAnswer": "",
                                "isSelected": "0",
                                "id": "b7c3bf52f63d43db84083daa66b11ce2",
                                "content": "The blood urea nitrogen and creatinine levels will continue to rise during the first few days of diuresis."
                            },
                            {
                                "isRight": "1",
                                "userAnswer": "",
                                "isSelected": "0",
                                "id": "99e4a92b0ffd4330b63dc98ffb98b638",
                                "content": "The diuretic phase is characterized by an increase in urine output of about 500 mL in a 24-hour period."
                            }
                        ],
                        "extJson": {
                            "optionList": [
                                {
                                    "isRight": "0",
                                    "userAnswer": "",
                                    "isSelected": "0",
                                    "id": "b1de32e20e124e82b0bc478e8e10e64b",
                                    "content": "The increase in urine output indicates the return of some renal function."
                                },
                                {
                                    "isRight": "0",
                                    "userAnswer": "",
                                    "isSelected": "0",
                                    "id": "a14168785aa748659a4f438d70fc0f26",
                                    "content": "The diuretic phase develops about 14 days after the initial insult and lasts about 10 days."
                                },
                                {
                                    "isRight": "0",
                                    "userAnswer": "",
                                    "isSelected": "0",
                                    "id": "b7c3bf52f63d43db84083daa66b11ce2",
                                    "content": "The blood urea nitrogen and creatinine levels will continue to rise during the first few days of diuresis."
                                },
                                {
                                    "isRight": "1",
                                    "userAnswer": "",
                                    "isSelected": "0",
                                    "id": "99e4a92b0ffd4330b63dc98ffb98b638",
                                    "content": "The diuretic phase is characterized by an increase in urine output of about 500 mL in a 24-hour period."
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        "isSubmit": "0",
                        "delFlag": "0",
                        "type": "0",
                        "version": 0,
                        "content": "The nurse instructor is evaluating a nursing student for knowledge regarding care of a client with acute kidney injury. Which statement by the student demonstrates the need for further teaching about the diuretic phase of acute kidney injury?",
                        "subjectId": "ad5ab04c79144c0ea9b83385308c3b1d",
                        "difficultyIds": "a0f7d178f7b728b8bba7ef20ddeca62d",
                        "difficulty": "考试范围-Evaluating 评价",
                        "textAnalysis": "Rationale:\nThe diuretic phase of acute kidney injury is characterized by an increase in urine output of more than 1000 mL in a 24-hour period. This increase in urine output indicates the return of some renal function; however, blood urea nitrogen and creatinine levels continue to rise during the first few days of diuresis. The diuretic phase develops about 14 days after the initial insult and lasts about 10 days.",
                        "id": "2084c1aca4214f44b221cfda05d1d210",
                        "mark": "0",
                        "collectionState": "0"
                    },
                    {
                        "optionList": [
